.\" La page de manuel de subedit-cli.
.\"
.\" **Courte, et c'est délibéré.** Le manuel complet vit sous
.\" share/subedit/manual et fait deux mille lignes ; le recopier ici en
.\" produirait une seconde copie à tenir, qui divergerait. Cette page dit ce
.\" qu'une page de manuel dit : la forme d'appel, ce que chaque sous-commande
.\" fait, les options globales, les codes de retour — puis où lire le reste.
.\"
.\" Le numéro de version est injecté par configure_file depuis le
.\" project(VERSION) de CMakeLists.txt. La liste des sous-commandes, elle, est
.\" confrontée à celle du binaire par check-installation.sh : une sous-commande
.\" ajoutée sans être écrite ici fait échouer la porte.
.TH SUBEDIT-CLI 1 "" "subedit @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
subedit\-cli \- read, inspect and retime subtitle files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B subedit\-cli
.RI [ global\ options ]
.I subcommand
.RI [ options ]
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B subedit\-cli
reads SubRip and WebVTT subtitle files in UTF\-8, recognising the format from
the content rather than the extension, and writes them back out shifted,
transformed, re\-timed or cleaned.
.PP
Every subcommand takes several files and treats each one independently: the
failure of one does not stop the others.
.PP
.B Nothing is ever written without an explicit destination.
The six subcommands that write take one of
.BR \-\-output ,
.B \-\-output\-dir
or
.BR \-\-in\-place ,
and refuse to run without one.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B inspect
Report what a subtitle file is made of. Writes nothing.
.TP
.B convert
Write a subtitle file out in another format or shape.
.TP
.B shift
Move every position of a file by a fixed amount.
.TP
.B transform
Correct every position from two points known to be right.
.TP
.B framerate
Re\-time a file mastered at one frame rate for another.
.TP
.B snap
Move every position onto the nearest frame of a frame rate.
.TP
.B hearing-impaired
Remove the sounds described between brackets or parentheses.
.PP
.BR framerate " and " snap
take the same arguments and do opposite things: the first re\-times a file
whose timing is wrong, the second moves each position onto the nearest frame of
a file whose timing is right. Getting one for the other raises no error.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
They may be written before or after the subcommand, indifferently.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit; the subcommand's own help if one is named.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Say nothing but errors.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-vv ", " \-vvv
Say more. One is the default; two adds what was recognised of each file; three
adds the bytes read and written, and every diagnostic the reading ran into.
.PP
.B \-\-quiet
together with
.B \-v
is refused: two opposite intentions are not arbitrated in favour of whichever
was written last.
.SH OUTPUT
The standard output carries the result and nothing else \(em the report of
.BR inspect .
The standard error carries everything else: the narration, the warnings and the
errors. That split is what lets a report be redirected without the account of
what happened going with it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error \(em unknown option, invalid value, forbidden combination. Detected
before any file is touched, so a batch is never left half done.
.TP
.B 2
No file could be treated.
.TP
.B 3
Some files were treated, others were not.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @SUBEDIT_MANUAL_DIR@
The complete manual, in Markdown. It is exhaustive where this page is not:
every option, every accepted value, every message, with a real example per
subcommand.
.SH SEE ALSO
The manual named above, and
.BR subedit\-gui ,
the window this program shares its core with. It has no page of its own: a
window is not described by a page of manual, and its chapters are in the manual
named above.
